## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of Kleene 3-valued semantics, constraint propagation, and backtracking strategy in a practical policy engine scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic (T > U > F)
- Propagation and backtracking on partial assignments
- Constraint forms: implication, conjunction, disjunction

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued logic: F=0, U=1, T=2
const V = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
const not = v => (v===V.T?V.F:(v===V.F?V.T:V.U));
const and = (a,b)=> Math.min(a,b);
const or  = (a,b)=> Math.max(a,b);
const implies = (a,b)=> or(not(a), b);
function evalConstraints(I1,I2,I3,I4){
  const c1 = implies(or(I1, not(I2)), I3);
  const c2 = implies(and(I2,I4), not(I1));
  const c3 = or(and(I1,I3), I4);
  return [c1,c2,c3];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 8 indicators with memoization? 
- How does pruning change if you require at least two U values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests concrete application of 3-valued logic to pipeline gating, not just theory. Requires constructing a satisfiable partial assignment and a propagation strategy that avoids prematurely forcing F.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F ordering; ¬, ∧, ∨, → semantics
- Backtracking/forward-chaining under partial info
- Satisfiability with Unknowns in real-world policy constraints

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple Kleene-like 3-valued evaluator for our constraints
const V = {T:2, U:1, F:0};
function not(x){ return x===V.T?V.F : x===V.F?V.T : V.U; }
function or(a,b){ return a>b? a:b; }
function and(a,b){ return a<b? a:b; }
function imp(a,b){ // a -> b is (!a) OR b
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

